      "name": "Event-driven sweep line",
      "idea": "Transform intervals into start/end events, sort events by position (with start events breaking ties before end events), then sweep through events maintaining coverage depth. When depth transitions from 0→1, start a new merged interval; when depth transitions from 1→0, close the current merged interval. This models the problem as coverage tracking rather than pairwise interval comparison."
    },
    {
      "name": "Graph connectivity (union-find)",
      "idea": "Model each input interval as a node in a graph, create edges between intervals that overlap or touch, then use union-find (disjoint set union) to identify connected components. Each component represents a set of intervals that must merge together. Extract the min start and max end from each component to produce the merged intervals. This frames merging as a connectivity problem rather than a sequential merge operation."
    }
  ]
}
